#{[#edb/ref 17592186049044 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060649} :activity/id #uuid "b3e816b9-b26c-5877-ab1b-44486bb0568d" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060931 :tutorial/content "The **determinant** of a general $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$\nis denoted $\\text {det} ({A})$ and computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ \nthe determinant is\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} - {\\color{red}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{5}} = 1.\n$$\n\nTo remember how to compute the determinant of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, you can imagine taking the product of the diagonal of the matrix, and then subtracting the product of the off-diagonal terms, as follows:\n\n![](images/25/2594f99e0f02241dc6d01f85c82923103df1d6fb4dda5ca03d438637f5178a28.png)\n\nLater, we will see that the determinant is related to the \"volume\" spanned by the row vectors or column vectors in a matrix. We will also see that the determinant can tell us about many other properties of a matrix. \n\nHowever, in this lesson, we will just focus on computing the determinant." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059557 :question/id #uuid "2b6e8845-a53d-510c-b44f-1b00271ee507" :question/math-academy-id "e-4434" :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(A),$ if $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =18-10 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix With Integer Entries"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059558 :question/id #uuid "b066b73a-c777-550c-9c6e-af8093bf7fab" :question/math-academy-id "e-4435" :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(A),$ if $\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}-(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix With Non-Integer Entries"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059750 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059559 :question/id #uuid "043a5e58-e1a0-50a5-b1be-d404e6262991" :question/math-academy-id "e-4436" :question/problem "If\n${A} = \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        2 & -1 \\\n        -3 & k\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack$\nand\n$\\det({A}) = 5$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =5 \\\\ 2k-3 & =5 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in an Expression Involving the Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix"}}] :activity/title "The Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059885}] [#edb/ref 17592186049125 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088524} :activity/id #uuid "e07e7ccb-9045-51ac-ad31-1220ff860794" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088893 :tutorial/content "Given the scalar $a,$ the **multiplicative inverse** of $a$ is defined as the scalar $a^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot a^{-1} = a^{-1} \\cdot a = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, the multiplicative inverse of $2$ is the number $2^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ because \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 =  2\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **inverse** of $A$ is defined as the $n \\times n$ matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^{-1} = A^{-1} \\cdot A = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the following $2\\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of the above matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo verify that the above matrix is in fact the inverse of $A,$ first observe that if we multiply $A$ by the above matrix $A^{-1},$ then we get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1 & -1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply $A^{-1}$ by $A,$ then we also get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A} & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe do not yet know how to find the inverse of a matrix. However, we can verify that one matrix is the inverse of the other by carrying out the same procedure as we did here." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087506 :question/id #uuid "4f4be86f-5a12-5fdf-b98e-2095b1faae8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4499" :question/problem "Using the method of direct verification, determine whether the matrix $B$ is the inverse of $A,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We must check whether $AB = BA = I_2.$\n\nComputing $AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B = A^{-1}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087507 :question/id #uuid "7ab2814b-dc93-569b-bdd6-618880da6e2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4500" :question/problem "Using the method of direct verification, determine whether the matrix $B$ is the inverse of $A,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we  check whether $AB = BA = I_3.$\n\nComputing $AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B = A^{-1}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088894 :tutorial/content "The number $0$ does not have an inverse for multiplication over the real numbers, because there's no such number $a$ that $a \\cdot 0 = 1.$ Instead, for every $a$ we have $a \\cdot 0 = 0.$\n\nSimilarly, not every matrix has an inverse.\n\n- If a matrix is not square, an inverse does not exist.\n\n- Some square matrices do not have an inverse. If a square matrix does not have an inverse, then it is said to be **singular**. An example of a singular matrix is the zero matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe will soon learn how to calculate the inverse of a square matrix, and we'll also soon learn how to determine when a square matrix does not have an inverse." :tutorial/title "Not Every Matrix Has an Inverse"}}] :activity/title "Introduction to the Inverse of a Matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087930}] [#edb/ref 17592186050825 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101668} :activity/id #uuid "c8c3c4ee-2322-5eb7-80b7-81716b081931" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101956 :tutorial/content "To multiply a matrix by a column vector, we multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product.\n\nFor example, suppose we would like to multiply \n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we write the multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe result of the multiplication is another column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ with the same dimensions as $\\mathbf{x}$.\nNow, we calculate the entries of the product (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $y_1,$ the $1$st component of $\\mathbf{y}$, we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find $y_2,$ the $2$nd component of $\\mathbf{y},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100688 :question/id #uuid "f31d614e-a00c-5502-8b23-e9214bc2ae5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4397" :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/cd/cd3816d9938d4bd65d0eaa5abcb3db329aed3827219a8cea0186ced2d5759880.png)\nand\n![](images/b9/b9c909115c604eb1a96463bdd31a71e77caa0a11d0098a8144afd798b50ae236.png)" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 5 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 7\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/6d/6d470181fba760868835a72c5d5cfec3c90f9bf7bf7a9d86860dc8d981517652.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a 2x2 Matrix by a Column Vector"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100689 :question/id #uuid "63f6d2d5-0654-569f-bd71-437c835a49d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4398" :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/1c/1c38a037a66a5f86fd38a2b9ea6226420e455d5ef66fe776af7548fb6724841f.png)\nand\n![](images/ff/ffec9e0757e75cad35bbec65fb15d07eb580274c8beb4a9b3337d31cf964ec1c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-2+0 \\\\ 2+0+0 \\\\ -1+4+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a 3x3 Matrix by a Column Vector"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100690 :question/id #uuid "c154ead3-0974-5e0d-9653-38c20756921e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4399" :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/52/528d30567626c47a051ea97c39cfe64a6bb4538d54b8a355d004457eca98d106.png)\nand\n![](images/e7/e7fe9e8a74f76dfec4fd92313fe84650f6006e3c54795fb06e3a255e4dbe8296.png)" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}a\\cdot 3+c\\cdot 2 \\\\ b\\cdot 3+d\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a+2c \\\\ 3b+2d\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix With Variable Entries by a Column Vector"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101957 :tutorial/content "An alternative, yet equivalent, procedure for multiplying a matrix by a column vector involves adding scaled columns of the matrix $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2c \\\\ 2d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a+2c \\\\ 3b+2d\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote \n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}$ \nthen we can also write the multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}={\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{u}_1 + {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{u}_2\n$$\n\nThe expression above is a **linear combination** of the vectors $\\mathbf{u}_1$ and $\\mathbf{u}_2,$ where the corresponding coefficients are given by the entries of the vector $\\mathbf{x}.$" :tutorial/title "Alternative Rule"}}] :activity/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column Vector" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101054}] [#edb/ref 17592186050190 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193457} :activity/id #uuid "2acc7e09-91cc-5ff3-8a20-8051f891cc61" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193941 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=1 \\\\ x-y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables. \n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n![](images/72/724119f2404af61332fcabbb5cda3522f9d338a4c6427084e5901e1ca7cd4498.svg)\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This will eliminate the variable $x,$ allowing us to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{x}+2y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{x}+y & =2 \\\\ (2y+y) & =(1+2) \\\\ 3y & =3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-y & =-2 \\\\ x-(1) & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1, 1).$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192585 :question/id #uuid "c700cc22-203a-5ad3-913c-e5eb507e22e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13995" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+4b=1 \\\\ 2a+b=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $a,$ multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$, \nand add the equations.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2},$ then the coefficient of $a$ will turn into $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the second equation.\n\n![](images/2b/2b0aa0d1dbfeb1e711bde6e7741fe5706aa126d71c44ae417d3c78d01b83547a.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{-2a}-8b & =-2 \\\\ \\cancel{2a}+b & =-5 \\\\ (-8b+b) & =(-2-5) \\\\ -7b & =-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $a,$ multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2},$ and add the equations*."} :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193459}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193459 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192586 :question/id #uuid "eb7f1f32-1a5c-526e-878b-8046c4feb8c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13997" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=2 \\\\ x-2y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$, \nand add the equations.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $4,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2},$ then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n![](images/0e/0e494f68d4859d46c3c369274e415f10a850c664343d41cce8dcfcfdd660e17c.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =2 \\\\ 2x-\\cancel{4y} & =8 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(2+8) \\\\ 5x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2},$ and add the equations*."} :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193460}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192587 :question/id #uuid "f20f46d9-b799-57a2-90bc-12e6593e8655" :question/math-academy-id "e-13999" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y=8 \\\\ x-2y=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n![](images/a7/a7e5f9f09d67dd14a4797e5675585973e3aafeb1ee96e9021d245f49ce9c1c7e.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2x}+3y & =8 \\\\ \\cancel{-2x}+4y & =6 \\\\ (3y+4y) & =(8+6) \\\\ 7y & =14 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 2.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y = 2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =-3 \\\\ x-2(2) & =-3 \\\\ x-4 & =-3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1, \\,y=2,$ which can be expressed as $(1,2).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192588 :question/id #uuid "990e3660-dfd7-50fa-b60b-0478ff02a073" :question/math-academy-id "e-14002" :question/problem "Find the solution $(u,v)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5u-6v=3 \\\\ -3u+2v=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $-6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3,$ then the $v$-coefficient will become $6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the first equation.\n![](images/e7/e701d299976539abdbbe6e9c311c2db14c23cca6817c5a3de5b5c05a95760d99.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5u-\\cancel{6v} & =3 \\\\ -9u+\\cancel{6v} & =-15 \\\\ (5u-9u) & =(3-15) \\\\ -4u & =-12 \\\\ u & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u = 3.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $u = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+2v & =-5 \\\\ -3(3)+2v & =-5 \\\\ -9+2v & =-5 \\\\ 2v & =4 \\\\ v & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u = 3, \\,v = 2,$ which can be expressed as $(3,2).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192905}]}
